.TH TRAIL-LINT 5 2026-10-17 "Peios trail 0.1.0"
.SH NAME
trail-lint \- Trail writing-rule configuration
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
trail.toml
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBtrail lint\fR reads a site's sources and reports each breach of its writing
rules, with the file, line, and column. Every rule is off until the site turns
it on, and every word list is the site's own, so Trail holds no house style of
its own.
.PP
The lint is a separate command. A build never runs it, so a site that doesn't
use it is unaffected.
.PP
.PP
.nf
trail lint path/to/site
.fi
.PP
Findings and totals go to stderr:
.PP
.PP
.nf
trail: guide.product/1--basics.topic/100--intro.md:12:41: "e.g.": use "for example" [words]
trail: warning: guide.product/1--basics.topic/100--intro.md:30:18: add a serial comma before the last item [serial-comma]
trail: 1 error, 1 warning
.fi
.PP
The exit status is \fB1\fR if any breach is an error, and \fB0\fR otherwise.
The
command-line reference has the details.
.PP
.SS "Turning rules on"
.PP
Rules are configured in a \fB[lint]\fR table in the root \fBtrail.toml\fR,
below every
plain key:
.PP
.PP
.nf
[lint]
rules = ["words", "spelling", "em-dash", "heading-levels", "line-length"]
line_length = 78
proper_nouns = ["Peios", "Registry Editor", "netd"]

[[lint.words]]
match = ["e.g."]
use = "for example"

[[lint.words]]
match = ["simply", "just", "of course"]
message = "don't condescend; cut it"

[[lint.words]]
rule = "spelling"
match = ["colour*", "behaviour*", "catalogue"]
message = "use the US spelling"
.fi
.PP
.TP
\fBrules\fR
Type: array of strings; The rules that run: built-in rules, and the \fBrule\fR
names of word lists. A name that matches no rule is an error.
.TP
\fBwarnings\fR
Type: array of strings; Rules that report warnings, not errors.
\fBserial-comma\fR always does.
.TP
\fBline_length\fR
Type: integer; The longest a prose line can be, in characters. Default 78.
.TP
\fBproper_nouns\fR
Type: array of strings; Names whose casing is fixed, for the title and heading
case rules. A name can be several words.
.TP
\fBwords\fR
Type: array of tables; Word lists.
.TP
\fBignore\fR
Type: array of tables; Rules that don't apply under a path.
.PP
A malformed \fB[lint]\fR table fails \fBtrail build\fR too, so a typo can't
hide until
someone runs the lint.
.PP
.SS "Word lists"
.PP
Each \fB[[lint.words]]\fR table bans some words or phrases:
.PP
.TP
\fBmatch\fR
Required. The words or phrases to find. They match whole words, ignoring case,
and a phrase matches across a line break. A trailing \fB*\fR matches any
ending: \fBcolour*\fR finds "colors" and "colored".
.TP
\fBrule\fR
The rule the list belongs to. Default \fBwords\fR. Give lists that should be
turned on and off separately their own rule names, such as \fBspelling\fR.
.TP
\fBuse\fR
What to write instead. The message says \fB"colour": use "color"\fR.
.TP
\fBmessage\fR
Why, when \fBuse\fR doesn't say it.
.TP
\fBcase_sensitive\fR
Match case exactly. With it, \fBmay\fR doesn't match the specification keyword
\fBMAY\fR, and \fBus\fR doesn't match "US".
.PP
.SS "What counts as prose"
.PP
The lint reads sources as written, without building them, so nothing a build
generates is checked. Prose is the text a reader sees, so these are never
checked as words:
.PP
.IP \(bu 2
code spans and code blocks;
.IP \(bu 2
raw HTML;
.IP \(bu 2
link destinations and URLs;
.IP \(bu 2
frontmatter keys.
.PP
An article's frontmatter \fBtitle\fR and \fBdescription\fR, and every
\fBtrail.toml\fR and
link file's \fBtitle\fR and \fBdescription\fR, are prose. The root
\fBtrail.toml\fR isn't
checked.
.PP
.SS "The built-in rules"
.PP
Each rule's name is what turns it on, appears in its reports, and suppresses
it.
.PP
.TP
\fBem-dash\fR
Reports: An em dash without a space on each side, and more than one pair of em
dashes in a sentence.
.TP
\fBdouble-hyphen\fR
Reports: \fB--\fR used as a dash: \fBa -- b\fR or \fBa--b\fR. A flag such as
\fB--force\fR is not a dash; it belongs in code formatting.
.TP
\fBcurly-quotes\fR
Reports: Typographic quotes and apostrophes: \fB‘ ’ “ ”\fR.
.TP
\fBinvisible-characters\fR
Reports: No-break, narrow no-break, figure, and zero-width spaces, word
joiners, and byte order marks, anywhere outside a code block.
.TP
\fBexclamation\fR
Reports: An exclamation point used as punctuation. Operators such as \fB!=\fR
don't count.
.TP
\fBampersand\fR
Reports: \fB&\fR in prose or a title. Bold text, which names a UI element
exactly as it appears, and the operator \fB&&\fR don't count.
.TP
\fBdouble-space\fR
Reports: Two spaces after a period.
.TP
\fBtitle-case\fR
Reports: A frontmatter, \fBtrail.toml\fR, or link file title not in title
case. The message gives the title as it should be.
.TP
\fBheading-case\fR
Reports: A heading not in sentence case: a capital after the first word.
.TP
\fBheading-punctuation\fR
Reports: A heading ending in \fB.\fR, \fB,\fR, \fB:\fR, \fB;\fR, \fB!\fR, or
\fB?\fR.
.TP
\fBheading-levels\fR
Reports: A \fB#\fR heading (the title is the page's only \fB#\fR), a skipped
level, and any heading below \fB###\fR.
.TP
\fBcode-language\fR
Reports: A fenced code block without a language. Use \fBtext\fR for none.
.TP
\fBshell-prompt\fR
Reports: A line starting with \fB$ \fR in a \fBsh\fR, \fBbash\fR, \fBshell\fR,
or \fBzsh\fR block.
.TP
\fBline-length\fR
Reports: A prose line longer than \fBline_length\fR.
.TP
\fBhex-case\fR
Reports: Lowercase digits in a \fB0x\fR literal in prose or a code span.
.TP
\fBserial-comma\fR
Reports: A list of three or more items without a comma before its final "and"
or "or". A warning.
.PP
Some rules are heuristics. They aim never to report what isn't a breach, and
so they miss some that are.
.PP
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBtitle-case\fR and \fBheading-case\fR\fR leave alone any word in
\fBproper_nouns\fR,
  and any word whose casing is its own: an acronym ("GXWI", "SIDs"), or a name
  with a capital or digit inside it ("IPv6"). A word after a colon starts a
  subtitle and can be capitalized. A lowercase name such as "netd" is fine at
  the start of a title or heading once it is in \fBproper_nouns\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBline-length\fR\fR exempts tables, code blocks, raw HTML, frontmatter,
and
  link reference definitions. A line is also exempt when it fits once its link
  destinations and URLs are taken out.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBhex-case\fR\fR looks inside code spans, unlike every other rule, because
a
  hexadecimal value in prose is usually written as code: \fB0x1F\fR. Code
blocks
  follow the code's own style and are not checked.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBserial-comma\fR\fR counts a comma-separated run as a list only when
every
  item but the first is at most three words. It also skips a run that starts
  with an introductory clause or phrase ("If the key exists, read it and write
  it"), contrasts ("\fBx\fR, not \fBy\fR or \fBz\fR"), gives examples ("such
as A or B"),
  or quotes. A missed list is better than a wrong report, so it's a warning.
.PP
.SS "Suppressing a rule"
.PP
When a page breaks a rule on purpose, such as a style guide quoting the words
it bans, suppress the rule rather than turning it off for the whole site.
.PP
For one line, put a comment on the line before it:
.PP
.PP
.nf
<!-- lint-disable-next-line words -->
- Don't: "In this article, we will discuss logon sessions."
.fi
.PP
For a stretch of a page, put comments around it:
.PP
.PP
.nf
<!-- lint-disable words -->
Never write a lowercase "may" in prose.
<!-- lint-enable words -->
.fi
.PP
Name one or more rules, or none for every rule. A comment can sit in a
blockquote (\fB> <!-- ... -->\fR) or be indented into a list item.
.PP
Put the comments between blocks, not inside a paragraph. A comment line ends
the paragraph it interrupts, which changes the page. The comments are
invisible on the page, but the page's
Markdown mirror shows them, because
mirrors show the source as written.
.PP
For a whole page or a whole book, name its path in \fB[[lint.ignore]]\fR:
.PP
.PP
.nf
[[lint.ignore]]
path = "guide.product/3--conventions.book"
rules = ["hex-case"]
.fi
.PP
The path is relative to the site root, and covers a file or everything under a
directory. With no \fBrules\fR, every rule is ignored there.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR trail (1),
.BR trail.toml (5)
.PP
